note={E. Summary.},
note={E1. Snapshot.},
note={E2. The study proposes a variable-flow operation model for medium-depth U-type ground source heat pump systems, which comprehensively considers the dynamic heat load of terminal buildings and the dynamic heat exchange process on the ground source side, and adopts a variable-flow method to match the dynamic changes in building heat load, reducing energy consumption..},
note={E3. Key findings.},
note={E4. The study found that the variable flow rate method reduces power consumption by 22.1{\%} compared to the constant-flow rate operation mode, and that the system's energy consumption and COP are affected by parameters such as backfill material, borehole length, and heating load..},
note={E5. The study finds that the variable-flow operation model can reduce the power consumption of the heat pump unit and the circulating pump, resulting in a total reduction in power consumption of 22.1{\%}. The model also prioritizes controlling fluid flow, and when the building heat load decreases, the fluid flow also decreases, reducing the system's heating capacity and avoiding energy waste..},
note={E6. The key findings include that increasing the drilling depth reduces the required flow rate, which significantly reduces the energy consumption of the circulating pump, and that operating the system with variable fluid flow can reduce energy consumption. Additionally, the study found that an increase in the building thermal load leads to a nearly linear decrease in the COP of the heat pump unit and an increase in total power consumption..},
note={E7. Circulating pump power consumption from 117,332 kW{\textperiodcentered}h to 56,548 kW{\textperiodcentered}h, resulting in a total reduction inpower powerconsumption consumptionfrom of 22.1{\%}.},
