note={E14. The methods used in the study include establishing an analytical solution heat transfer model for medium-depth U-type ground heat exchangers and simulating the system's performance under different conditions. The study also uses a variable-flow operation model to comprehensively consider the dynamic heating load and heat transfer process of BHEs..},
note={E15. Results.},
note={E16. The results show that the variable flow rate method can reduce power consumption and improve energy efficiency, and that the system's performance is affected by various parameters such as backfill material, borehole length, and heating load..},
note={E17. The study finds that the variable-flow operation model can reduce the power consumption of the heat pump unit and the circulating pump, resulting in a total reduction in power consumption of 22.1{\%}. The average COP of the heat pump unit is 5.14, which is higher than the value of 4.99 with constant flow rate. The study also finds that the thermal conductivity of the backfill material has a significant impact on the system's performance, with higher thermal conductivity resulting in higher COP and heating guarantee rate..},
note={E18. The results show that increasing the drilling depth from 2300 m to 2500 m decreases the power consumption of the circulating pump from 85,844 kW{\textperiodcentered}h to 56,548 kW{\textperiodcentered}h. The study also found that operating the system with variable fluid flow can reduce energy consumption, with the average COP of the heat pump increasing from 4.99 to 5.12..},
note={E19. Conclusions.},
note={E20. The study concludes that the proposed analytical model and operation optimization model can provide guidance for the design and optimization of U-shaped ground source heat pump systems..},
note={E21. The study concludes that the variable-flow operation model is an effective way to optimize the performance of medium-depth U-type ground source heat pump systems, reducing energy consumption and improving the system's efficiency. The study also concludes that the thermal conductivity of the backfill material has a significant impact on the system's performance, and should be carefully considered in the design and operation of the system..},
note={E22. The conclusions drawn from the study are that increasing the drilling depth and operating the system with variable fluid flow can improve the system's performance and reduce energy consumption. The study also found that the thermal conductivity of the backfill materials and the building thermal load have a significant impact on the system's performance.},
doi={10.3390/en17133184},
url={https://doi.org/10.3390/en17133184}
}

@Article{bordignon2024evaluation,
author={Bordignon, Sara
and Pengo, Riccardo
and De Carli, Michele
and Zarrella, Angelo},
title={Evaluation of short and long-term performance of a dual-source heat pump},
year={2024},
keywords={ground-source heat pump; Coefficient of Performance; heat exchanger; TRNSYS; heat source},
abstract={A dual-source heat pump is a heat pump coupled with two heat sources/sinks, such as the ground and the air. Such solutions allow to balance the thermal load to the ground, shifting part of the heating or cooling thermal load to the finned coil heat exchanger and preventing the possible degradation of the long-term ground-source heat pump performance. In addition, a similar configuration can be more economically convenient than a ground source heat pump. Depending on the plant configuration and the boundary conditions (mainly climatic area), the control strategy to switch between the two heat sources and sinks is crucial to ensure good heat pump performance in the short and long-term operation of the system. This work investigates a control strategy to choose the value of switch temperature, which establishes the most favorable heat source or sink to be used by the heat pump. A dynamic model is developed using TRNSYS to study and predict the thermal and electrical behavior of double-source heat pumps, which can be used in the context of retrofitted and new buildings under different weather conditions. The modeled system simulates an existing commercial building, where a dual-source heat pump is installed and whose operation data are monitored.},
note={C. Highlights.},
note={C1. Towards the electrification of the building sector, using ground-source heat pumps can lead to an improvement in energy performance and a reduction in operating costs compared to traditional air-source heat pumps.},
note={C2. This work proposes a methodology for dynamically evaluating the best heat source or sink to use in a dual-source heat pump system.},
note={C3. The dynamic model was applied to a real case study, where boundary conditions, like the number of borehole heat exchangers and climatic data, were set.},
note={C4. The results of the parametric analysis applied to this case study show the economic inconvenience of fully balancing the thermal load to the ground.},
note={C5. Such operation leads to higher system performance in heating mode, it cannot compensate for the decrease in cooling performance. In this case, environmental aspects due to the ground thermal stress should be considered. This approach is based on a long-term performance evaluation, and allows to dynamically adjust the switch temperature to reduce the electrical consumption of the dual-source heat pump and to prevent the ground thermal drift.},
note={C6. The possibility to dynamically adapt the settings according to changes that might occur in real applications, such as an unexpected increase in thermal load due to extreme weather conditions, or increase/decrease in the heated or cooled areas, is extremely interesting.},
note={D. Findings.},
note={D1. The models are reported in Eq (3) and Eq (4) and presented a RMSE lower than 1{\%} compared with catalog data.},
note={D2. The models presented a RMSE lower than 2{\%}, Although the models still depend on the source and load sides entering and exiting water temperatures, a new term has been introduced, which is the partial load ratio (and its square), calculated as the heating (or cooling) capacity in partial load divided by the nominal heating (or cooling) capacity declared by the manufacturer.},
note={D3. In particular, the equation has the same shape as Eq 6, where SEFT is the temperature of the external ait. For this model, the RMSE is lower than 4{\%} compared to manufacturet's data.},
note={E. Summary.},
note={E1. Snapshot.},
note={E2. This study investigates a control strategy for a dual-source heat pump (DSHP) to optimize its performance by selecting the most favorable heat source/sink, with the goal of minimizing thermal unbalance to the ground and improving energy efficiency..},
note={E3. Key findings.},
note={E4. The study aims to investigate the performance of a DSHP system and the impact of the control strategy on its energy efficiency. The results of the study will provide insights into the optimal control strategy for a DSHP system..},
note={E5. The study finds that fully balancing the thermal load to the ground may not be the most economical option, as it can lead to higher electrical consumption..},
note={E6. A key finding is that a poor initial choice of parameters can significantly impact the results, and that dynamic adaptation of settings can help mitigate issues such as unexpected increases in thermal load due to extreme weather conditions..},
note={E7. Such operation leads to higher system performance in heating mode, it cannot compensate for the decrease in cooling performance. In this case, environmental aspects due to the ground thermal stress should be considered. This approach is based on a long-term performance evaluation, and allows to dynamically adjust the switch temperature to reduce the electrical consumption of the dual-source heat pump and to prevent the ground thermal drift.},
note={E8. The possibility to dynamically adapt the settings according to changes that might occur in real applications, such as an unexpected increase in thermal load due to extreme weather conditions, or increase/decrease in the heated or cooled areas, is extremely interesting.},
note={E9. Objectives.},
note={E10. The objective of the study is to investigate a control strategy for a DSHP system to optimize its performance by selecting the most favorable heat source/sink. The study aims to minimize thermal unbalance to the ground and improve energy efficiency..},
note={E11. Methods.},
note={E12. The study uses a dynamic model developed with TRNSYS to simulate the thermal and electrical behavior of a DSHP system. The model is calibrated using manufacturer's data and validated with monitored data from a commercial building. A parametric analysis is carried out to evaluate the best strategy for selecting the heat source/sink..},
note={E13. The study uses a dynamic model of the DSHP system, which takes into account the thermal load to the ground, the performance of the heat pump, and the electrical consumption of the auxiliaries..},
note={E14. The methods used in the study include parametric analysis to evaluate the impact of different parameters on the system's performance..},
note={E15. Results.},
note={E16. The results of the study show that the control strategy has a significant impact on the performance of the DSHP system. The study provides insights into the optimal control strategy for a DSHP system..},
note={E17. The results of the parametric analysis show that the variable switch temperature case leads to higher electrical consumption (+0.5{\%}) compared to the baseline case, due to a generally lower Seasonal Performance Factor (SPF) in cooling..},
note={E18. The results show that dynamic adjustment of the switch temperature can help reduce electrical consumption and prevent ground thermal drift..},
note={E19. Conclusions.},
note={E20. The study concludes that the control strategy for a DSHP system has a significant impact on its performance and energy efficiency. The study provides insights into the optimal control strategy for a DSHP system and highlights the importance of minimizing thermal unbalance to the ground..},
note={E21. The study concludes that the proposed methodology can be used to evaluate the best heat source or sink to use in a DSHP system, and that fully balancing the thermal load to the ground may not be the most economical option..},
note={E22. The conclusions of the study highlight the importance of considering environmental aspects and ground thermal stress in the evaluation of the dual-source heat pump's performance.}
}

@Misc{cervera2016control,
